\begin_layout Abstract
Large-scale implicit-feedback music recommendation systems operate under conditions of extreme catalog scale, severe interaction sparsity, and heterogeneous signals spanning latent collaborative patterns, social tag semantics, and temporal recency dynamics. When merging heterogeneous retrieval streams across a catalog exceeding one million items, differences in score dispersion and dynamic range across users frequently lead to suboptimal ranking fusions. In this paper, we present a Global Normalized Multi-Stream Arbiter architecture evaluated on the complete Last.fm 1K dataset (
\begin_inset Formula $19,150,865$
\end_inset

 raw interaction events across 
\begin_inset Formula $1,493,688$
\end_inset

 unique candidate tracks) enriched with HetRec 2011 artist-level tag semantics (
\begin_inset Formula $2,299$
\end_inset

 vocabulary tags covering 
\begin_inset Formula $594,225$
\end_inset

 catalog items). The architecture integrates three distinct predictive signals: (1) an implicit Modified Singular Value Decomposition (MSVD) via alternating least squares with log-confidence scaling, (2) an artist-level TF-IDF tag semantic affinity stream, and (3) a time-decayed temporal recency preference stream. To resolve score scale incompatibility without allocating dense user-item matrices, we introduce per-user min-max normalization prior to global weighted multi-stream fusion. Evaluation is conducted under a strict, chronologically ordered, zero-leakage split across 
\begin_inset Formula $518$
\end_inset

 evaluable test users over the full 
\begin_inset Formula $1.49\text{M}$
\end_inset

-track candidate catalog. On the complete test set, the Global Normalized Arbiter achieves an NDCG@10 of 
\begin_inset Formula $0.007414$
\end_inset

, representing a 
\begin_inset Formula $+16.384\%$
\end_inset

 descriptive improvement over Base MSVD (
\begin_inset Formula $0.006370$
\end_inset

), a 
\begin_inset Formula $+244.648\%$
\end_inset

 statistically significant improvement over the Popularity baseline (
\begin_inset Formula $0.002151$
\end_inset

, Wilcoxon 
\begin_inset Formula $p = 0.0447$
\end_inset

), and a 
\begin_inset Formula $+24.480\%$
\end_inset

 nominal improvement over unnormalized fixed fusion (
\begin_inset Formula $0.005932$
\end_inset

). Cohort analysis reveals that the multi-stream signals provide a 
\begin_inset Formula $+50.472\%$
\end_inset

 descriptive NDCG@10 gain (
\begin_inset Formula $0.017421$
\end_inset

 vs.
\begin_inset space \space{}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $0.011578$
\end_inset

) for low-activity users (
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

–
\begin_inset Formula $2,090$
\end_inset

 training plays). Exact sparse top-
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 retrieval executes in 
\begin_inset Formula $96.48\text{ ms}$
\end_inset

 per user query (
\begin_inset Formula $10.36\text{ queries/sec}$
\end_inset

 per CPU core) with verified sparse memory safety. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Keywords
Recommender systems, implicit feedback, matrix factorization, alternating least squares, multi-stream fusion, score normalization, temporal preference decay, Last.fm, HetRec. 
\end_layout

