\end_layout

\end_inset

 recommendation systems in music streaming services operate in an environment characterized by extreme catalog dimensionality, non-negative observational feedback, and pronounced temporal and semantic variability
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
key "hu2008collaborative,celma2008music"
literal "false"

\end_inset

. In platforms such as Last.fm, users interact with vast catalogs containing millions of tracks, but individual consumption profiles cover only a minute fraction of available items, resulting in matrix sparsity exceeding 
\begin_inset Formula $99.99\%$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Modern recommender systems typically leverage latent factor models, such as Matrix Factorization solved via Alternating Least Squares (ALS) with implicit confidence transformations
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
key "hu2008collaborative,koren2009matrix"
literal "false"

\end_inset

. While collaborative filtering effectively identifies global behavioral co-occurrences, it faces three fundamental structural challenges when deployed in isolation at extreme scale: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Cold-End Interaction Sparsity:
\series default
 Users with limited interaction histories lack sufficient collaborative density for accurate latent factor estimation. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Temporal Non-Stationarity:
\series default
 User consumption exhibits strong recency dynamics, short-term listening bursts, and preference drift over time
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
key "koren2010collaborative"
literal "false"

\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Score-Scale Incompatibility in Hybrid Systems:
\series default
 Combining latent collaborative dot-products with external knowledge sources (e.g., social tag profiles) and temporal affinity vectors is impaired by divergent score distributions and non-standardized dynamic ranges across different users. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To address these challenges, we introduce a 
\series bold
Global Normalized Multi-Stream Arbiter
\series default
 recommendation framework designed for large-scale, full-catalog ranking. Our approach formulates top-
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 track recommendation as the unified arbitration of three complementary streams: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Collaborative Factorization Stream:
\series default
 An Implicit Modified Singular Value Decomposition (MSVD) model mapping users and tracks into a 64-dimensional latent space via confidence-weighted ALS. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Stream A (Artist-Tag Semantic Affinity):
\series default
 A content-based retrieval stream matching user listening history to artist-level social tags from the HetRec 2011 Last.fm dataset
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
key "cantador2011hetrec"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 using TF-IDF weighting and cosine projections. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Stream B (Temporal Recency Preference Modeling):
\series default
 An exponentially decayed interaction affinity stream that captures immediate recency bias relative to pre-evaluation reference timestamps. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A central contribution of this work is the analysis and implementation of 
\series bold
per-user score normalization
\series default
. Naive linear fusion of unnormalized collaborative scores and auxiliary scores causes score dominance or severe ranking distortion due to variance in latent dot-product magnitudes across dense versus sparse users. We demonstrate that performing per-user min-max normalization on the collaborative stream prior to global fusion resolves this scale mismatch.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Crucially, we evaluate our system under a 
\series bold
strict chronological zero-leakage protocol
\series default
 over the entire 
\begin_inset Formula $1,493,688$
\end_inset

-item candidate catalog using 
\begin_inset Formula $19,150,865$
\end_inset

 listening events from the Last.fm 1K dataset
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
key "celma2008music"
literal "false"

\end_inset

. Unlike sampled evaluations that evaluate against artificial subsets of negative items
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
key "cremonesi2010performance"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, our evaluation ranks all 
\begin_inset Formula $1.49\text{M}$
\end_inset

 candidates for each evaluable test user. Furthermore, we provide a complete statistical audit utilizing non-parametric Wilcoxon signed-rank tests
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
key "wilcoxon1945individual"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 10,000-sample bootstrap confidence intervals
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
key "efron1994introduction"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, establishing rigorous empirical boundaries for all observed performance gains.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Related Work
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Implicit Feedback Collaborative Filtering
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Implicit feedback (e.g., listening counts, clicks, dwell time) differs fundamentally from explicit ratings because non-interactions represent an unobserved mixture of negative preference and lack of exposure
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
key "steck2010training,liang2016modeling"
literal "false"

\end_inset

. The seminal work of Hu, Koren, and Volinsky (HKV)
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
key "hu2008collaborative"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 formulated implicit matrix factorization by introducing binary preference variables 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{ui} \in \{0, 1\}$
\end_inset

 coupled with confidence weights 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{ui} = 1 + \alpha r_{ui}$
\end_inset

. The resulting least-squares objective with L2 regularization is solved efficiently via Alternating Least Squares (ALS) with an 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}(f^2 |R| + f^3 |I|)$
\end_inset

 per-iteration complexity, where 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is latent dimensionality. Extensions such as Bayesian Personalized Ranking (BPR)
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
key "rendle2012bpr"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 optimize pairwise ranking criteria directly. In this work, we employ an implicit MSVD/ALS formulation utilizing logarithmic confidence scaling 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{ui} = 1 + \kappa \ln(1 + r_{ui})$
\end_inset

 to stabilize confidence estimation across heavy-tailed listening play counts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Tag-Aware and Content-Augmented Recommendation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Social tagging systems allow users to annotate artists, albums, and tracks with descriptive keywords. The HetRec 2011 benchmark
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
key "cantador2011hetrec"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 established standardized artist-level social tag assignments from Last.fm, enabling vector-space modeling of artist genres, eras, and stylistic descriptions. Prior literature has explored tensor factorization
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
key "rendle2012bpr"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and hybrid linear models to incorporate tag information into collaborative filtering. However, when scaling to millions of candidate items, many tracks lack rich track-level annotations. Propagating artist-level tags across candidate tracks via TF-IDF representations provides an informative auxiliary semantic channel, provided the system accounts for tag sparsity and vocabulary dispersion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Temporal Dynamics in Recommender Systems
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
User preferences in music streaming exhibit multi-scale temporal non-stationarity, ranging from short-term repetition to long-term drift
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
key "koren2010collaborative"
literal "false"

\end_inset

. Koren
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
key "koren2010collaborative"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 introduced TimeSVD++, parameterizing user and item biases as continuous functions of time. In fast-paced listening domains, exponential recency decay applied directly to interaction play counts provides a robust, non-parametric mechanism to prioritize recent musical affinity without requiring complex non-linear parameter estimation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Multi-Stream Fusion and Score Calibration
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Hybrid recommendation architectures combine heterogeneous retrieval models (collaborative, content, popularity, session)
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
key "sarwar2001item"
literal "false"

\end_inset

. A critical bottleneck in hybrid fusion is score calibration. Unnormalized collaborative dot-products 
\begin_inset Formula $x_u^T y_i \in (-\infty, \infty)$
\end_inset

 vary dramatically across users depending on vector norms and interaction density. In contrast, cosine-based tag similarity scores are bounded in 
\begin_inset Formula $[0, 1]$
\end_inset

, and temporal affinity scores scale with decay functions. Without per-user normalization, global linear blending parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum w_m = 1$
\end_inset

 fail to produce uniform trade-offs across distinct user activity cohorts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Problem Formulation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{U} = \{u_1, u_2, \dots, u_{|\mathcal{U}|}\}$
\end_inset

 denote the set of users and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{I} = \{i_1, i_2, \dots, i_{|\mathcal{I}|}\}$
\end_inset

 denote the full candidate item catalog. The system observes an implicit event log 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D} = \{(u, i, t, r_{ui})\}$
\end_inset

, where each record represents user 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 playing track 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 at timestamp 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 with aggregate count 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{ui} \in \mathbb{N}_{\ge 1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For a target user 
\begin_inset Formula $u \in \mathcal{U}$
\end_inset

 evaluated at test time, let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_u \subset \mathcal{I}$
\end_inset

 denote the set of items consumed by user 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 during training and validation periods. The objective is to produce an ordered recommendation list of top-
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 candidate tracks 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{Top-}K(u) \subset \mathcal{I} \setminus \mathcal{H}_u$
\end_inset

 that maximizes ranking utility over the future ground-truth test interaction set 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{T}_u$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\text{Top-}K(u) = \operatorname*{arg\,max}_{S \subset \mathcal{I} \setminus \mathcal{H}_u, \, |S|=K} \sum_{i \in S} \hat{r}_{ui},
\end{equation}
\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{r}_{ui}$
\end_inset

 is the multi-stream scoring function combining collaborative, tag semantic, and temporal recency affinity.
\end_layout

