. In the low-activity cohort, where latent collaborative factor estimation is most severely impaired by sparse feedback, the Global Normalized Arbiter achieves an NDCG@10 of 
\begin_inset Formula $0.017421$
\end_inset

, compared to 
\begin_inset Formula $0.011578$
\end_inset

 for Base MSVD. This represents a substantial 
\begin_inset Formula $+50.472\%$
\end_inset

 relative descriptive improvement. Auxiliary tag semantic and temporal signals provide vital anchor points when user interaction histories contain fewer than 
\begin_inset Formula $2,090$
\end_inset

 plays.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Conversely, for high-activity users (
\begin_inset Formula $>5,028$
\end_inset

 plays), Base MSVD maintains strong collaborative factor representations (
\begin_inset Formula $0.004634$
\end_inset

 NDCG@10), whereas aggressive auxiliary blending can introduce semantic over-concentration (
\begin_inset Formula $0.002407$
\end_inset

 for the Arbiter). We emphasize that while the multi-stream arbiter provides effective auxiliary signals for sparse, low-activity users within the existing catalog, it does not solve zero-data unseen-item cold start.
\end_layout

