\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Limitations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We explicitly acknowledge the following limitations of this study: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Catalog Sparsity and Absolute Metric Scale:
\series default
 In a 
\begin_inset Formula $1.49\text{M}$
\end_inset

-item catalog with 
\begin_inset Formula $518$
\end_inset

 test users, ground-truth hits per user are extremely sparse, yielding low absolute metric values across all evaluated models. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Tag Vocabulary Coverage:
\series default
 HetRec tags cover 
\begin_inset Formula $39.7824\%$
\end_inset

 of candidate tracks (
\begin_inset Formula $594,225$
\end_inset

 items). Tracks by unannotated artists receive zero semantic score mass. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Artist-Level Semantic Granularity:
\series default
 Tags are assigned at the artist level, meaning track-specific stylistic variations are not distinguished. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Absence of Audio/Acoustic Modeling:
\series default
 The system does not utilize waveform, acoustic, or deep audio features. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Offline Observational Evaluation:
\series default
 Evaluation is based on observational logs without live online A/B testing or user feedback loops. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Scope of Cold Start:
\series default
 The system does not solve zero-data unseen-item cold start where items have no artist tags or historical interactions. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Future Work
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Future investigations will explore: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Integrating track-level audio embeddings derived from self-supervised music audio transformers. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Extending the arbiter with non-linear neural gating networks trained under listwise ranking losses. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Incorporating session-based graph neural networks for sub-daily listening transitions. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Conclusion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This paper presented a Global Normalized Multi-Stream Arbiter recommendation system evaluated on the full Last.fm 1K dataset across 
\begin_inset Formula $1,493,688$
\end_inset

 candidate tracks. By introducing per-user min-max normalization, the architecture successfully reconciles implicit collaborative MSVD factorizations, artist-level HetRec tag TF-IDF profiles, and temporal recency decay signals. Evaluated under a strict zero-leakage chronological protocol, the Global Normalized Arbiter achieved an NDCG@10 of 
\begin_inset Formula $0.007414$
\end_inset

, establishing a 
\begin_inset Formula $+16.384\%$
\end_inset

 descriptive gain over Base MSVD, a 
\begin_inset Formula $+244.648\%$
\end_inset

 statistically significant gain over Popularity (
\begin_inset Formula $p = 0.0447$
\end_inset

), and a 
\begin_inset Formula $+50.472\%$
\end_inset

 descriptive improvement for low-activity users, while supporting exact top-
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 retrieval in 
\begin_inset Formula $96.48\text{ ms}$
\end_inset

 per query with verified sparse memory safety.
\end_layout

